Z||000000||fotofrank||10-19-2001||09:59 AM|||| I have an '84 SE with an oil leak  or spray. It seems to be near the distributor.<BR>Could this be a distributor seal or something else. It really doesn't leak but sure is "blown" out of some where. What do you think? TIA<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||5
Z||000001||K2m4||10-19-2001||10:55 AM||||hey foto...the dukes are known for leaking around the valve cover and do drip everywhere near where your talking. Ive owned many fieros and still own 3 of them and all my dukes leak oil...nothing that a little degrease and a garden hose can take care of<P>kerm <BR>86 2m4<BR>86 GT<BR>88 2m4<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||10-19-2001||11:09 AM||||I'd reseal the valve cover...use RTV sealant instead of a gasket...the haynes manual shows how to properly do this.<P>Also be sure PCV plumbing and valve are good...also check the breather. If the PCV system isn't working properly, the blowby and oil will find the route of least resistance, which might be through the valve cover sealing area. And this can really make a mess, and is dangerous.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fotofrank||10-19-2001||12:49 PM||||I've sealed the valve cover with blue RTV. The "leak" seems to be coming from the area that sprays on the plug wires at the distributor.I have not investigated with the car running but oil is on the transmission in front( be hind) the engine bloce near the distributor. It could be a blown gaskit for the head. I'll have to look while the cars is running.<BR>Also when i stip for a red light and accelerate when green comes on the engine stumbles and sounds like only 2 or 3 cylenders are fireing. after a few seconds ( maby 5) the engine is again strong. Coul oil be screw up the plugs?||||reg||8
Z||000004||Banzai||10-19-2001||04:59 PM||||My duke was covered in oil from top to bottom.  I replaced all gaskets.  Sounds like it could be the ouput seals in the tranny.  Mine were shot and there was oil being flung all over the place.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||10-19-2001||05:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fotofrank:<BR><B> I have an '84 SE with an oil leak  or spray. It seems to be near the distributor.<BR>Could this be a distributor seal or something else. It really doesn't leak but sure is "blown" out of some where. What do you think? TIA<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Have you changed the <B>oil filter</B> lately?<BR>Take it back off and make sure you don't have two gaskets. My Duke spit all its oil out because I had two oil filter gaskets, it was spraying out between them.<P>If that's not it check the oil sending unit, mine was bad and leaking but not as bad as you are talking about.||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||10-19-2001||05:43 PM||||When my '84 4cyl had a bad valve cover and lifter cover gasket, the oil showed up mostly on the area where the eng mounts to the trans. I believe there is also an 'o'ring on the distributor, but this shouldn't put oil on your plug wires. Valve cover gaskets get hard and won't compress anymore on high mileage engs, or engs that have a overheating history. As stated above, check the pcv plumbing & valve. If the eng isn't vented properly, oil will be forced out everywhere it can by crancase pressure. Also a possibility that the drainback holes in the top of the head are plugged with crud from blowby, even the area around the pushrods. been there already.<BR>It was a mess, & ended up rebuilding when I let some of the crud fall down into the pan and clog the pump screen. Oil leaks are the one of the culprits behind the infamous fires on this model Fiero. Get it fixed!<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-19-2001).]||||reg||1
